 $k$ for which the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 15 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455448 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4468650e-bb61-59b4-9610-7643cc731b43" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455448 #edb/ref 1074 "The vector $v$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T)$ if there exists a vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]$  such that $T(u)=v.$\n\nTherefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot u & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-2 & 7 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 15 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-2x+7y \\\\ -3x+9y \\\\ 4x-5y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 15 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x+7y=12 \\\\ -3x+9y=15 \\\\ 4x-5y=k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x=3y-5$, which we substitute into the first equation to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+7y & =12 \\\\ -2(3y-5)+7y & =12 \\\\ y & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3y-5 \\\\  & =3(2)-5 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=1$ and $y=2$ into the third equation and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-5y & =k \\\\ 4(1)-5(2) & =k \\\\ k & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $v\\in \\text{Im}(T)$ when $k=-6.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455450 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186455384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455450 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81241" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455450 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455450 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x+y \\\\ -3x+6y \\\\ 2x+5y\\end{aligned}]$ be a linear transformation. Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -3 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455450 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "467dee5d-03ae-509f-b883-503eb8bc0e16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455450 #edb/ref 1074 "The vector $v$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T)$ if there exists a vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]$ such that $T(u)=v.$ \n\nTherefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x+y \\\\ -3x+6y \\\\ 2x+5y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -3 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y=k \\\\ -3x+6y=-3 \\\\ 2x+5y=20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x=2y+1$, which we substitute into the third equation to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+5y & =20 \\\\ 2(2y+1)+5y & =20 \\\\ 9y & =18 \\\\ y & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2y+1 \\\\  & =2(2)+1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=5$ and $y=2$ into the first equation and obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & =k \\\\ 5+2 & =k \\\\ k & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $v\\in \\text{Im}(T)$ when $k=7.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455464 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186455387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455464 #edb/ref 1069 "q-38612" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455464 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455464 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $T=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 10 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ be the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455464 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6964e08a-7b43-5dcf-b2e7-f5084c3a12db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455464 #edb/ref 1074 "The vector $v$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T)$ if there exists a vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]$  such that $T(u)=v.$\n\nTherefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot u & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 10 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4x-2y \\\\ 2x-8y \\\\ 6x+10y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-2y=2 \\\\ 2x-8y=8 \\\\ 6x+10y=k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x=4+4y$, which we substitute into the first equation to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-2y & =2 \\\\ 4(4+4y)-2y & =2 \\\\ 14y & =-14 \\\\ y & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4+4y \\\\  & =4+4(-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=0$ and $y=-1$ into the third equation and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+10y & =k \\\\ 6(0)+10(-1) & =k \\\\ k & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $v\\in \\text{Im}(T)$ when $k=-10.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455507 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186455353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455507 #edb/ref 1069 "q-38620" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455507 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455507 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}w \\\\ x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}w-x+2y-z \\\\ x-y-z \\\\ 2w+x-3y+2z \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ find its rank." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455507 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ebab4da7-6448-59a5-b164-1d32d42c04ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455507 #edb/ref 1074 "First, the standard matrix of $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Im}(T))=\\text{rank}(T).$ \n\nSo, we need to find the rank of the matrix $T.$ We reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -7 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $T$ has $3$ pivot columns, we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=3.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455513 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186455350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455513 #edb/ref 1069 "q-38004" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455513 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455513 #edb/ref 1071 "The standard matrix $T$ of a linear transformation $T$ and the vector $b\\in {R}^{3}$ are given below. Determine if there exists a vector $x\\in {R}^{3}$ such that $T(x)=b.$\nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2},$ where ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ are the components of $x.$  \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ -6 & 8 & -7 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455513 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fbd847cd-d4b3-5c82-bd9e-da0f4f3b9e2d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455513 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $T$ is the standard matix of $T,$ we have $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T).$ In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Im}(T),$ we need to check if the equation $Tx=b$ is consistent. \n\nSo, we find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[T\\,|\\,b],$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 4 & -10 \\\\ -6 & 8 & -7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 4 & -10 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 & -27 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 4 & -10 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 & -27 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 28\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get the false statement $0=28.$ Therefore, the system is inconsistent, and we conclude that  $b\\notin \\text{Im}(T).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
